\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The inversion test at a reflected site}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Give fields on a finite periodic grid values in a
finite-dimensional complex Hermitian space #{S\otimes F}.
Choose self-adjoint Euclidean directions on #{S}, an axis #{t}
and a constant self-adjoint \vocabk{grading}{fdip-013N}
#{\Gamma} on values. Write #{u=\varepsilon_t\otimes\bbI_F}
and suppose #{u\Gamma=-\Gamma u}. Let #{h} fix coordinate #{t}
and negate the others. The inversion #{(\mathcal P\psi)(x)
=u\psi(hx)} exchanges the sign spaces. Give these spaces
unitary group actions #{\rho_\pm} at the source and
#{\rho'_\pm} at the target, constant over sites.
For a prescribed #{\alpha:G\to G'} it intertwines the actions
precisely when, for both signs and every #{g\in G},
##{u_\pm\rho_\pm(g)=\rho'_\mp(\alpha(g))u_\pm,}
where #{u_\pm:V_\pm\to V_\mp} are the value restrictions.
For an internal action #{\bbI_S\otimes\rho_F} with the same
action at the target and #{\alpha=\mathrm{id}}, equivariance
holds whenever the sign spaces are stable.}

\p{Let #{A_\pm(x;U,\phi)} be the blocks of an even onsite
coefficient for a represented connection #{U} and scalar field
#{\phi}. Prescribe destination scalar data #{\phi'} and
destination blocks #{A'_\pm}. Put
##{U^{\mathcal P}_t(x)=U_t(hx),\qquad
 U^{\mathcal P}_j(x)=U_j(hx-e_j)^{-1}\quad(j\ne t),}
where #{e_j} is one positive step. The inversion transports the
whole onsite coefficient to the specified destination if and
only if, at each #{x} and with both sign choices,
##{u_\pm A_\pm(hx;U,\phi)
 =A'_\mp(x;U^{\mathcal P},\phi')u_\pm.}}

\proof{
\p{The value map is a unitary involution. Anticommutation maps
each #{\Gamma}-eigenspace onto the opposite one. Pullback
permutes sites and commutes with constant value actions. On a
field supported at #{hx}, testing group equivariance at #{x}
gives exactly the two displayed restrictions, and those
restrictions suffice at every site. The internal tensor-factor
action commutes with #{u} on all values.}

\p{The source edge over #{x\to x+e_t} runs forward from #{hx}.
For #{j\ne t} it runs backward to #{hx-e_j}, so the stored
positive transport there must be inverted, as in
\ref{fdip-013T}. Acting on a field supported at #{hx} also
gives the two coefficient identities by \ref{fdip-018F}.
Every field is the sum of its site-supported parts.}
}
